# One so far, and it was found by `--sabotage` rather than by reading: the module
# was emptied and this file kept passing, on **both** lanes. A test that passes
# against a module that does nothing is measuring something else.
#
#   test-child-process-fork-closed-channel-segfault.js
#
# Its subject is `cluster`, which this profile does not implement. It calls
# `cluster.isPrimary` and forks through node's own cluster, so `child_process`
# never comes into it -- and because `cluster` is not in this module's `uses`, the
# runner hands it node's real one, which makes the file node-against-node. What it
# asserts is the absence of a segfault, which an absent module satisfies for free.
#
# Removing it lowers this module's count from 81 to 80 on the interpreted lane and
# from 1 to **0** on the compiled lane. That second number is the honest one: the
# compiled lane has no non-hollow pass here at all, and reporting 1 was reporting
# this file.
